These three independent functional fixes address incorrect rumble voltage
selection and strength changes in pm8xxx-vibrator. The affected code is
present at input master/next 8e16f8bbf1bd528975fd6f4081eaf00513c1d83e.

The drv2 address contains the peripheral base even on variants without a
second drive field, so testing that address chooses the wrong voltage
range. The inclusive scaling formula also exceeds the maximum at full
speed. Finally, ORing the new drive level into the cached register retains
old drive bits when an effect changes strength without stopping. The
patches select the range from the register layout, clamp the endpoint, and
replace the drive field. Each patch includes its introducing Fixes commit.

Validation:
- Clang 21.1.8 ARM W=1 object builds pass for the unmodified base and all
  three prefixes, with KCFI and full compressed DWARF5. No C compiler
  diagnostics. The adapted Fx0 config produces two olddefconfig notices
  converting old modular IPV6/NETFILTER_NETLINK settings to current types.
- All five actual-C host fixtures are byte-identical to the earlier
  qualified stable fixtures: 768 ASan/UBSan speed/variant cases pass, and
  the original plus three independent regression controls fail. Those
  unchanged tests are reused rather than rerun. PMI632 upper-byte encoding
  is outside their coverage; no fix for that behavior is claimed here.
- Strict checkpatch passes each patch with zero findings; only the DCO
  sign-off check is disabled for these unsigned drafts. Every exported
  prefix replays to the expected complete Git tree.
- Earlier signed Fx0 PM8226 RAM tests exercise the same fixed voltage
  functions: active codes 24 -> 17 -> 22, then off and module removal. They
  use the existing Fx0 integration kernel and board support. Other PMIC
  variants have host coverage only. This current maintainer kernel has
  not been fully linked, packaged or booted, and GCC was not tested.

No driver prerequisites are needed to apply the series. Hardware testing
requires the separately maintained Fx0 integration support. The current
tree's devm_kzalloc_obj allocation API is retained unchanged. Human review,
DCO certification, and any additional maintainer-requested testing remain.

